title: b3.1 Memory, conversation and trust
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Eight concepts. Why walk: fresh air and movement stimulate memory and steady emotions (HFC-01). What walking costs: talking takes attention from the path (HFC-02). How conversations recover: self-repair first (HFC-03), on common ground (HFC-04). Why partners miss each other's slips, and how a System can model them: theory of mind (HFC-05). What to protect: face (HFC-06). What slips cost the resident: withdrawal and loneliness (HFC-07). Why no deception: trust (HFC-08).

= Human Factors Concept - Fresh air, movement and memory [HFC-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-01
|Name|Fresh air, movement and memory
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature and one group observation (hypothesis: moments on foot are remembered better than moments in a chair; Open item O-1).
|Concept statement|Fresh air and movement stimulate memory and steady emotions. Side by side, on the move, talk comes easier and a pause costs less.
|Human factor addressed|Memory; mood; conversation
|Proposed mechanism|Walking supports the hippocampus. Time in nature lowers rumination. Places give talk a subject, and a way back after a slip. Walking side by side asks for no eye contact.
|Source|Erickson et al. (2011): a year of walking enlarged the hippocampus in older adults. Forbes et al. (2015): exercise in dementia. Bratman et al. (2015): a nature walk lowered rumination. Evans & Jones (2011): walking interviews draw more place-bound talk.
|Conditions and population of validity|Mostly healthy and older adults; dementia evidence weak.
|Predicted Effect type|More pleasure on the walk; more talk; calmer afterwards
|Associated Measures|M-02, M-03, M-05

= Human Factors Concept - Walking while talking [HFC-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-02
|Name|Walking while talking (dual task)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Talking takes attention from walking. Older people with cognitive impairment slow, stop or lose balance under a second task, and notice tiredness later.
|Human factor addressed|Gait; fall risk; body awareness
|Proposed mechanism|Gait and speech draw on the same limited attention. A question demands more than a statement. Engaged talk crowds out signals of fatigue.
|Source|Lundin-Olsson et al. (1997): older people who stop walking to talk fall more often. Montero-Odasso et al. (2012): gait slows and varies under a second task in cognitive impairment.
|Conditions and population of validity|Established in older adults; stronger with cognitive impairment.
|Predicted Effect type|Stumbles while talking; walks running past the set length
|Associated Measures|M-06

= Human Factors Concept - Conversation repair [HFC-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-03
|Name|Conversation repair
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Speakers prefer to fix their own slips. In dementia, slips become more frequent and repair succeeds less often.
|Human factor addressed|Conversation; face
|Proposed mechanism|Self-repair needs a moment. Help offered too soon takes the turn and shows the slip. A cue to the last topic, or a new one, lets the talk go on.
|Source|Schegloff et al. (1977): preference for self-repair. Orange et al. (1996): repair in Alzheimer's disease.
|Conditions and population of validity|Conversation analysis of healthy talk; small samples in Alzheimer's disease.
|Predicted Effect type|Wait-then-redirect raises recovered slips without more embarrassment
|Associated Measures|M-04

= Human Factors Concept - Common ground and reminiscence [HFC-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-04
|Name|Common ground and reminiscence
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Conversation rests on what both people know. Old memories outlast recent ones and give a pair common ground.
|Human factor addressed|Conversation; identity
|Proposed mechanism|Partners forget what they share. The System knows it from both profiles and may offer it, with consent. A shared topic from early adulthood is recalled with least effort.
|Source|Clark & Brennan (1991): grounding in communication. Rubin et al. (1998): early adulthood is recalled best. Woods et al. (2018), Cochrane review: small, setting-dependent benefits of reminiscence.
|Conditions and population of validity|Reminiscence studied in groups, not on walks; benefits small.
|Predicted Effect type|Talk restarts after a slip; more positive interaction
|Associated Measures|M-04, M-05

= Human Factors Concept - Theory of mind [HFC-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-05
|Name|Theory of mind
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature and group reasoning.
|Concept statement|Understanding what others know, believe and feel weakens in dementia: two residents often miss each other's slips. A System can keep a simple model of each walker.
|Human factor addressed|Social cognition; conversation
|Proposed mechanism|Per walker, for one walk: common ground, the thread, what was said already, current beliefs, state (speaking, searching, finished, upset, tuned out). The model decides when to wait, redirect or defer.
|Source|Bora et al. (2015), meta-analysis: theory of mind impaired in Alzheimer's disease. Scassellati (2002): theory of mind for a humanoid robot.
|Conditions and population of validity|The walker model is a design construct; machine theory of mind shown only in narrow tasks.
|Predicted Effect type|Slips noticed that the partner missed
|Associated Measures|M-01 (model against listener codes), M-04

= Human Factors Concept - Face and personhood [HFC-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-06
|Name|Face and personhood
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Each interaction affirms or erodes a person's standing. Corrections, quizzing and sing-song speech (elderspeak) erode it.
|Human factor addressed|Experienced dignity; withdrawal
|Proposed mechanism|Help addressed to one person marks them as failing. Addressed to both, as a statement, it marks nobody.
|Source|Goffman (1967): face-work. Kitwood (1997): personhood. Sabat (2001): the self in Alzheimer's disease. Williams et al. (2009): elderspeak provokes resistance.
|Conditions and population of validity|Care-staff speech; robot voices not studied.
|Predicted Effect type|Embarrassment, withdrawal; descriptions of being talked down to
|Associated Measures|M-04 (face signs), M-09 (code T)

= Human Factors Concept - Coping, withdrawal and loneliness [HFC-07] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-07
|Name|Coping, withdrawal and loneliness
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Aware of decline, people protect themselves: some keep going as before, some face it openly. Avoiding talk protects face and leads to loneliness. Compensation lets a person keep doing what they value.
|Human factor addressed|Coping with ageing and cognitive loss; loneliness
|Proposed mechanism|The System keeps the thread outside the person's head: compensation. How openly it helps follows the person's coping style.
|Source|Clare (2003): coping in early Alzheimer's disease. Baltes & Baltes (1990): selection, optimisation and compensation. Victor (2012): loneliness in care homes. Holwerda et al. (2014): loneliness predicted dementia onset.
|Conditions and population of validity|Coping studied in early dementia, at home. Loneliness measures in dementia of uncertain validity.
|Predicted Effect type|More willingness to walk with others; less loneliness over weeks
|Associated Measures|M-07, M-08

= Human Factors Concept - Truth and trust [HFC-08] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-08
|Name|Truth and trust
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Literature only.
|Concept statement|Lies told to spare distress are common in dementia care, and contested. Found out, they cost trust. A blunt truth from a stranger causes grief.
|Human factor addressed|Trust; grief
|Proposed mechanism|Trust builds on consistent, truthful answers from known people. Gentle redirection answers the feeling without saying anything untrue.
|Source|Mental Health Foundation (2016): inquiry on truth and lying in dementia care. James et al. (2006): staff lying, and guidelines.
|Conditions and population of validity|UK care practice; System-delivered answers not studied.
|Predicted Effect type|Trust kept; fewer repeated painful questions
|Associated Measures|M-09 (code U), M-10

